# A language server, as a subsystem.
#
# `launch` is the only machine-specific line, and it is the whole
# configuration: point it at the server you have.  The stub below speaks the
# real protocol and needs nothing installed, so a fresh checkout works.
# Replace it with one of these and restart the shell:
#
#     launch python -m pylsp          (python-lsp-server; the one that has been
#                                      run through this adapter end to end)
#     launch rust-analyzer            (not installed on this machine: the
#                                      ~/.cargo/bin shim needs the rustup
#                                      component)
#     launch clangd
#     launch pyright-langserver --stdio
#     launch typescript-language-server --stdio
#
# `{here}` in a launch line is the directory this descriptor's module lives in,
# which is how the stub is found from any workspace.  A real server is named
# exactly as you would type it in a terminal.
#
# The server is started the first time the client asks it something
# (`autostart yes`), because a server nobody has asked a question is a process
# nobody wanted, and it is stopped when the shell exits.
id lsp
title Language server
kind process
module lsp.mjs
capabilities completion diagnostics hover definition format
autostart yes
launch node "{here}/lsp-stub.mjs"
description LSP client: completion, diagnostics, hover and definition from a real language server
